---
/**
 * Step 2 of the homepage hierarchy: why conventional primary care usually
 * cannot deliver the experience the hero describes.
 *
 * Copy rule for this section: the constraint is structural, never personal.
 * It is about panel math and reimbursement, not about bad physicians or a
 * named health system. Keep it that way if this copy is ever revised.
 */
---

<section class="problem section" aria-label="Why this is hard to find">
  <div class="container problem-inner">
    <span class="section-label">Why This Is Hard to Find</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Most doctors want to give you this. The system rarely lets them.</h2>
    <div class="divider"></div>

    <p>
      A typical primary care physician is responsible for two to three thousand
      patients. To keep a practice solvent under insurance reimbursement, that
      schedule has to move in ten and fifteen minute increments, which means the
      visit routinely ends before the conversation does. That is not a failure of
      skill or of caring. It is what the arithmetic of the model requires.
    </p>
    <p>
      The downstream effects are familiar to almost everyone. You wait weeks for an
      appointment, then explain your history to someone reading it for the first
      time. Your questions get routed through a portal or a front desk. Care gets
      divided among specialists who never quite talk to one another, and no single
      physician is holding the whole picture. Prevention, which takes time and
      repetition, becomes whatever fits in the minutes that are left.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this is fixable one visit at a time. It is fixable by changing the
      arithmetic. Fewer patients per physician, visits long enough to finish the
      conversation, and a practice funded directly by the people it cares for
      rather than by billing volume. That is the entire idea behind Forward Family
      Medicine.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

